Linux provides a command-line interface (CLI) that allows users to interact with the operating system using commands. This section covers the basic commands used to identify the current user, navigate through the filesystem, view files and directories, check command history, and access command documentation.

👤 whoami — Know Your User

The whoami command shows the username of the user currently logged in.

whoami
📍 pwd — Know Your Location

pwd stands for Print Working Directory. It shows the path of the directory I am currently working in.

pwd
📂 ls — Explore Files

The ls command lists files and directories in the current location.

ls
ls -l
ls -la
🚶 cd — Navigate

cd stands for Change Directory and is used to move between directories.

cd Documents
cd ..
cd /
cd -
🕐 history — Review Commands

The history command displays commands that I previously executed in the terminal.

history
history 10
📖 man — Learn from Documentation

man stands for Manual. It helps me understand what a command does and what options are available.

man ls
man chmod
💡 What I Learned

These commands gave me the foundation for working with Linux. I learned to identify myself, understand where I am, explore the filesystem, navigate between directories, review my work, and find command documentation.
